_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 1
---
order: 1
---
short_label: XII.1
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Similar polygons inscribed in circles are to one another as the squares on their diameters.
----
proof:

Let {ABC|cABC} and {FGH|cFGH} be circles, let {ABCDE|pABCDE} and {FGHKL|pFGHKL} be similar polygons inscribed in them, and let {BM} and {GN} be diameters of the circles.

I say that the square on {BM} is to the square on {GN} as the polygon {ABCDE|pABCDE} is to the polygon {FGHKL|pFGHKL}.

Join {BE}, {AM}, {GL}, and {FN}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="440" height="250" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XII.1 diagram" src="propXII1.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The similar pentagons, their diameters BM / GN, and the two pairs of
    // equiangular triangles: ABE / FGL by VI.6, then ABM / FGN through the
    // III.27 angles at M and N and the III.31 right angles at A and F; VI.4
    // makes the diameters proportional to the sides, and VI.20 squares both.
    //
    // Joyce's figure draws the two circles, the two pentagons, the diameters
    // and the four joins, all kept as they are. The joins BE, AM, GL, FN and
    // the diameters are declared from the letter Joyce names first, so their
    // own strokes run the right way and need no twins. Everything added is
    // transparent: the sides BA, AE, GF, FL, the four triangles (explicit ;0
    // faces), the arcs AB / FG the angles at E, M and L, N stand on, and
    // faceless twins of both circles and both pentagons (cABC, cFGH,
    // pABCDE, pFGHKL). The originals have opaque faces and a highlighted
    // face is raised (#140) over the angle markers inside it, so every
    // circle or polygon highlight, here and in the prose, goes to a twin.
    //
    // Markers: equal angles share a colour — BAE / GFL blue; AEB, AMB, FLG,
    // FNG green (one chain of equalities); the right angles BAM / GFN red
    // squares (III.31); the remaining angles ABM / FGN orange. At A and F the
    // right angle nests inside the pentagon's angle (auto-stepped rings).
    var GIVEN = ["ABC","FGH","A","B","C","D","E","F","G","H"];
    var POLY  = GIVEN.concat(["ABCDE","FGHKL","K","L"]);
    var DIAM  = POLY.concat(["BM","GN","M","N"]);
    var JOIN  = DIAM.concat(["BE","AM","GL","FN"]);
    var SIDES = JOIN.concat(["AB","AE","FG","FL"]);
    var ANG1  = SIDES.concat(["angBAE","angGFL"]);
    var TRI1  = ANG1.concat(["ABE","FGL"]);
    var ANG2  = TRI1.concat(["angAEB","angFLG"]);
    var ARC   = ANG2.concat(["angAMB","arcAB"]);
    var ARC2  = ARC.concat(["angFNG","arcFG"]);
    var RT    = ANG2.concat(["angAMB","angFNG","rtBAM","rtGFN"]);
    var REM   = RT.concat(["angABM","angFGN"]);
    var TRI2  = REM.concat(["ABM","FGN"]);
